the united nations secretary general is  calling for a diplomatic solution to the  north korean crisis the u.s response to  pyongyang's nuclear threat is the  subject of this morning of our ongoing  series issues that matter south korea's  navy conducted live fire drills  yesterday to show its military force  after north korea's largest nuclear test  sunday the u.s wants stricter sanctions  on kim jong-un's regime as his nation  possibly prepares for a new missile test  former defense secretary ash carter  spent more than three decades working on  national security issues in both  republican and democratic  administrations he was involved in  military planning during the 1994 crisis  over north korea's nuclear program he's  now the director of the belfast center  at the harvard kennedy school and joins  us this morning ash carter this is  certainly your territory so we're very  curious to see what you're thinking  these days you've heard secretary mattis  say this thank you that the u.s would  respond to any threat by north korea in  his words massive military response  sounds very scary sounds very intense  what type of threat would warrant that  type of response what does that mean  when you hear that  well we've been prepared and continue to  prepare for decades for north korean  aggression we are very well prepared  it's important that we protect our  people  as well as our friends and allies from  north korea i think at this juncture  what i would advise is a  strategy of coercive diplomacy you know  people talk about is it diplomacy or is  it military action in general in  strategic affairs and certainly in this  case what you need is a mix  in which you  require north korea to take steps  progressively to reduce the threat  or you take steps progressively to in  military and economic sanctions  to apply pressure to them if down the  road they continue to make the wrong  choice  and this road it does in fact lead to  military conflict we have had the time  first of all  better to prepare our forces even than  the considerable preparation that they  have now  our allies japan and south korea and  also to sideline japan and those are  necessary prep preparations for  effective military uh action but i think  at this juncture it's coercive diplomacy  the mix of the military and the  diplomatic that's the package you just  said  but you just said if they make the wrong  choice what is the wrong choice that  would warrant massive military action  what do we need to see some people say  firing these test missiles is a very  disconcerting thing  well they're all disconcerting it's been  going on for quite some time i think  that uh what we need them to do is  halt  underground testing of nuclear weapons  halt  uh tests of ballistic missiles not fire  ballistic missiles of long range in our  direction or the direction of our allies  and at each step of the way they can  either take that step in back from this  brink or toward the brink in either case  we will be prepared either to relax  pressure on them or to increase pressure  on them and as you say  down the road  after if they continue to make bad  choices uh military action may be  necessary i think secretary mattis is  pointing to our readiness to do that and  the possible necessity to do uh that um  but after coercive diplomacy plays out  in this way we are better prepared uh if  that eventuality occurs which it will  make and as you know the question has  been whether china and russia will  engage in that course of diplomacy into  the way that that may be needed  president trump is having a phone call  this morning with the chinese president  xi jinping is it time for the chinese to  cut off the oil supply to the north  well absolutely it's been  for some time  in what i would regard as china's best  strategic interest in the long run to  apply its ability to essentially  strangle north korea increase  importantly  through energy to get them to  stop doing something which could lead to  a war  on china's border we will win that war  uh but it is a very very unpleasant war  for us but also for china however as you  say i've been observing this for decades  now uh and it's hard to be optimistic  because the chinese have consistently  disappointed so i think we also need to  prepare ourselves and prepare our  friends and allies and if the chinese  sideline themselves  then  that's unfortunate for them unfortunate  for us but we will be prepared to  protect our people and our friends and  allies secretary how did we get here in  the first place i know privately some  feel the obama administration was too  restrained  on north korea would you agree with that  assessment  well this has been going on for decades  it goes back as as you pointed out  multiple administrations of both parties  the chinese uh the rather than north  koreans first  tested nuclear weapons in 2006  they were launching ballistic missiles  in the 1990s uh so it's been getting  worse and uh  do you think more should have been done  than under the last two years of the  bush administration and then the eight  years of the obama administration do you  think more could have been done well i  certain  i i certainly think we had some success  some limited success in the 1990s this  was mentioned earlier with something  which was essentially coercive diplomacy  a step uh step-by-step progressive  intensification of pressure or  relaxation of pressure depending upon  whether north korea took steps in the  right direction i still think that's the  right way to go  it's a mixture of diplomacy and military  and as i said if that  if the  north koreans continue to make bad  choices  it will lead uh in all likelihood to  greater military action but we will be  better prepared we will position more  forces  on the korean peninsula that may be  necessary that's been discussed  sanctions you've mentioned uh important  also providing advanced systems to our  allies south korea and japan because  they will be an inevitable part of any  military action because remember the  north koreans hate them as much as they  hate us and they threaten them and  they're right there and they're our  friends  and allies and so there are ways in  which through this process we can both  give a try uh to the possibility of of  diplomacy succeeding but also position  ourselves progressively in a strength  strengthened posture  if and when military action is required  which it  may well be ash carter thank you very  much joining us from harvard kennedy  schools uh belfor so we appreciate your  time
 

